{"retractions" [[17592186156927 :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$"] [17592186156927 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$ whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" []}
